works: On the fourth day the lights come back on, and the street that came through it together keeps waving across the road.
works: {N} comes out of the dark week with a new respect for how much {N} can manage without.
fails: The outage drags into a second week, and the cold, the dark and the empty shelves wear everyone down.
fails: The dark days cost more than anyone expected: spoiled food, lost wages, and an old neighbour taken to hospital.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a crash takes the savings (pairs D4a)

== a crash takes the savings
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-100
holds: savings
per_year: 0 0 .02 .03 .03 .03
drivers: prosper-.8 unrest+.4 era+.2 trouble+.15
gap: 6-20
times: per person with savings: deep falls in share prices of a third or more come about every 10 to 15 years, and a bank or fund failure that takes deposits is rarer (estimate leaning on the big falls in world share prices of recent generations); about half of such crashes cut a saver's own savings hard enough to be a turning point, so about 3 in 100 savers a year in the working and retired years, 2 in 100 young adults, who have less invested; 'a recession' (read) is the slump in jobs and shops that often follows, and 'financial ruin' the bankruptcy that can come after; with the world on, the Engine fires it on a financial crash in its economy (spec-1, spec-5 economy events)
likelier: savings in shares, a pension fund or a weak bank, a boom that ran too long, unrest, a crisis abroad that travels along trade links
rarer: savings spread over safe places, deposits the state guarantees, a calm economy
at: bank
tone: trouble
life: money, home
horizon: months
roles: partner, parent, child, friend, colleague
earth: the markets fall by a third in a week, a bank shuts its doors, and the savings of years shrink overnight
scene: It starts on a Monday. By Thursday the markets have fallen by a third, one bank has shut its doors, and there is a queue outside {Ns} own branch before it opens. The savings of years are worth half what they were on Sunday.
scene W: {N} did everything right: paid in every month, never touched it, trusted the people who said it was safe. Somebody broke a promise somewhere.
scene U: {N} spreads the statements across the table and works out exactly how much has gone, and how much is only gone on paper.
scene B: Everyone is selling in a panic, which means everything is for sale cheap. {N} feels the fear, and under it a cold interest.
scene R: {N} wants to shout at someone, anyone: the bank, the news, the smiling man on the screen who said it could never happen.
scene G: {parent} lost the savings once before, in another crash, and planted potatoes the next spring. {N} remembers the potatoes.
- W | keep paying in every month as planned, and trust the long run | v: security, conformity | self_control: + | lever: loyalty | pushes: economy | chance: 65
- U | work out what went wrong, and spread what is left over safer places | v: security, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 70
- B | sell up on a tip from inside the bank, before the news breaks | v: security, power | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: trading on a secret tip; backfire: investigated, fined, and named in the papers | lever: subvert | pushes: economy | chance: 70
- R | march on the bank's head office with the other savers, and refuse to leave | body: light | v: self-direction, universalism | mark: defied an authority | lever: voice | pushes: institution bank | chance: 55
- G | stop opening the statements, and live on less until it passes | v: security, tradition | lever: neglect | pushes: economy | chance: 65
- BG | buy cheap while everyone else is selling, and wait as long as it takes | v: power, achievement | self_control: + | takes_if_fails: savings | chance: 55
- RG | take what is left out of the bank in cash, and keep it at home | v: security | self_control: - | lever: exit | pushes: institution bank | chance: 85
